#pragma once

// UniverseDensity.ush
// The ray march, and nothing else. Everything that touches a texture -- warp,
// regional variance, the analytic web, the cluster/gas composition -- now lives in
// UniverseDensityCore.ush's SampleAtPosition, which returns a single finished density
// per sample. This file's job is purely the sphere-bounded march: step sizing,
// accumulation, early-out, and calling that one function once per step.
//
// The texture and sampler are threaded down into the core rather than sampled here, same
// as before. THE FIELD OFFSET NO LONGER PASSES THROUGH THIS FUNCTION AT ALL: it is
// constant for the draw, so it is resolved once into the params struct instead of being
// handed to every sample. It still has to stay split into an integer cell count and a
// fraction -- a float3 carrying the player's position stops resolving a fraction of a
// cell long before the magnitudes this layer reaches.
//
// Nothing here touches Parameters or View -- those exist only inside the generated
// material function, so the dither that needs them stays in the Custom node body.

#include "/UltraLargeScale/UniverseDensityCore.ush"

// --- RAYMARCH: ANALYTIC SPHERE-BOUNDED DENSITY ACCUMULATION ---
// Same stepping rule as the galaxy layer. The proxy mesh decides which pixels run; the
// unit sphere decides where to march.
//
// SAMPLES SIT AT STEP MIDPOINTS, and the bounds fade is applied here rather than in the
// core -- see both notes in the loop.
//
// STEP LENGTH IS SET BY THE MARCH, NOT BY THE CAMERA. The first step is
//
//     baseStep = chord / StepCount
//
// and every step after it grows linearly with how far along the chord it starts:
//
//     h = baseStep * (1 + StepGrowth * t01)
//
// This replaces sizing the step as a fraction of camera distance, and the reason is the
// near field. A camera-distance step goes to ZERO at the camera, so a viewer inside the
// volume -- which is the normal case here, since the proxy is a sphere centred on them
// -- got a step floored only by an absolute pin, and successive samples landed inside a
// single cell, each paying a full fifty-four-candidate neighbourhood walk to re-derive a
// field that had not changed between them. A first step derived from the chord cannot
// collapse that way, because it does not know where the camera is.
//
// The two rules agree about the far field and disagree only at the origin, which is
// worth knowing before tuning: with the camera at the centre of its own proxy, dCam is
// about t, so the old rule was 2*k*t -- linear in distance, through zero -- and the new
// one is affine in distance with a floor the plan chose. The growth term is what keeps
// the far half from being oversampled.
//
// STEPCOUNT IS A BUDGET, NOT A COUNT. Growth means the steps overshoot the chord before
// the budget is spent, so the loop finishes early: the actual count is
// StepCount * ln(1 + growth) / growth, which is 0.69 of the budget at growth 1 and 0.46
// at growth 3. Raising growth trades far-field detail for step count directly.
//
// Two floors sit under all of it. The authored MinStep, and the field's own resolution,
// which stops the step going below the width of the narrowest wall the parameters can
// produce. They answer different questions -- how far a step may go, and how much
// detail is actually there to find.
//
// Do not add a box bounds test on the marched position. InStartPos sits exactly on
// the proxy surface, so a component is exactly 0 or 1 before any FP error, and such a
// test breaks on iteration zero wherever rounding pushes it outside -- view-direction
// dependent, so it shows as angle-dependent dropout at silhouettes.
//
// WILL CHANGE: the universe proxy is a sphere centred on the CAMERA, so the chord is
// a constant length and the entry/exit maths collapses to a fixed span rather than an
// intersection. Correct as written for a fixed test volume; not the final bounding.
//
// InViewVec is the LOCAL camera vector, surface toward camera. DIRECTION ONLY.
//
// InCamPosN is the camera in NORMALIZED space -- the same frame as the marched position,
// box spanning -1 to 1 -- and no remap is applied to it here. A remap applied in the
// shader silently biases anything supplied in a different frame, and a biased camera
// position is indistinguishable from a correct one until the geometry is wrong.
//
// Once the proxy becomes a sphere centred on the camera this collapses: the camera is
// always at the origin, InCamPosN is zero, and the distance is just t.
//
// RETURNS  (emission, steps taken, 0, transmittance).
//
// The step count rides in G because cost has no other observer: a march that truncates
// looks identical to one that finished, and the far half of the volume is simply absent.
float4 UniverseRayMarch(UniverseDensityParams P, Texture3D InTex, SamplerState InSampler,
                        float3 InStartPos, float3 InViewVec, float3 InCamPosN,
                        float InStepGrowth, float InMinStep, float InStepCount,
                        int InMaxSteps, float InDensityScale, float InJitter,
                        float InNoisePower)
{
    // Unit box [0,1] -> normalized space [-1,1]. Uniform scale, so a direction is
    // identical in both.
    float3 o = 2.0 * (InStartPos - 0.5);
    float3 dir = normalize(-InViewVec);

    float b = dot(o, dir);
    float c = dot(o, o) - 1.0;
    float disc = b * b - c;
    if (disc <= 0.0)
    {
        return float4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    }

    float sq = sqrt(disc);

    // Origin inside the sphere: clamp the near end. Outside: take the whole chord
    // WITHOUT clamping to zero, because with an inverted-normal proxy the shaded
    // point is the far face and the chord lies at negative t.
    float tEnter = (c < 0.0) ? 0.0 : (-b - sq);
    float tExit = -b + sq;
    if (tExit <= tEnter)
    {
        return float4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
    }

    // DISTANCE FROM THE CAMERA. It no longer sets step size -- see the header -- and its
    // only remaining job is trimming the chord below, so that a camera inside the volume
    // does not march the stretch behind itself. The sample at t sits at o + dir*t and the
    // camera at o - dCam*dir, so their separation is (dCam + t).
    float dCam = dot(o - InCamPosN, dir);

    // START AT THE CAMERA WHEN IT IS INSIDE THE VOLUME. The chord otherwise begins
    // behind the viewer and the budget is spent on geometry nobody can see -- and this
    // matters MORE under plan-relative stepping than it did under camera-relative, since
    // the step no longer collapses over that stretch but is sized as if it were part of
    // the visible span.
    //
    // GUARDED SO IT CAN ONLY SHORTEN. Each condition rules out a way a wrong InCamPosN
    // could empty the chord instead of trimming it; the fallback is the full chord,
    // which is slower but still correct.
    float tStart = tEnter;
    if (dCam > 0.0 && -dCam > tEnter && -dCam < tExit)
    {
        tStart = -dCam;
    }

    // THE CHORD IS WHAT SIZES THE STEP. Everything about the progression falls out of
    // these two lines: the span the ray actually has to cover, divided by the budget.
    float span = max(tExit - tStart, 1e-6);
    float baseStep = span / max(InStepCount, 1.0);

    // FLOORED BY THE FIELD'S OWN RESOLUTION as well as by the pin. P.MinFeatureStep is
    // half the narrowest wall the field can produce, in normalized units, derived from
    // the feature width and the small cell size together -- see
    // UNIVERSE_MIN_STEP_FRACTION in the core. The authored pin cannot do this job alone:
    // it is an absolute distance with no knowledge of the cell size, so it is wrong the
    // moment CellSizeRange moves.
    //
    // Taking the LARGER of the two leaves the pin able to make steps coarser and stops it
    // making them pointlessly finer. It binds only when the budget is set high enough
    // that baseStep falls below what the field can express, which is exactly when it
    // should.
    float minStep = max(max(InMinStep, P.MinFeatureStep), 1e-5);

    float growth = max(InStepGrowth, 0.0);
    float invSpan = 1.0 / span;

    // Dither shortens the FIRST step. It costs no path length -- the loop clamps the
    // last step to the exit -- and it offsets each pixel's lattice independently.
    float firstScale = clamp(InJitter, 0.05, 1.0);

    float t = tStart;
    float stepsTaken = 0.0;

    bool bShape = (InNoisePower != 1.0);

    // Cell units to normalized units.
    float transmittance = 1.0;
    float emission = 0.0;

    // InMaxSteps is a SAFETY BOUND, not the step count. StepCount and the growth rate
    // set the count between them; this only stops a pathological combination from
    // hanging the shader, and should sit comfortably above StepCount rather than near it.
    [loop]
    for (int i = 0; i < InMaxSteps; i++)
    {
        if (t >= tExit)
        {
            break;
        }

        stepsTaken += 1.0;

        // THE STEP IS SIZED BEFORE THE SAMPLE IS TAKEN, because the sample sits at the
        // MIDPOINT of the step and the midpoint is not known until the length is. That
        // ordering is the only structural difference from sampling at the step start.
        //
        // GROWS WITH PROGRESS ALONG THE CHORD, not with distance from the camera. t01 is
        // saturated rather than trusted: tStart can be trimmed forward to the camera
        // above, and a wrong InCamPosN that trimmed it past tExit would otherwise drive
        // the step negative and hang the march.
        float t01 = saturate((t - tStart) * invSpan);
        float h = max(baseStep * (1.0 + growth * t01), minStep);

        // FIRST-STEP DITHER. Without it every ray starts at the same place and takes
        // identical steps, so sample positions lock to the field across every pixel at
        // once -- structured aliasing that swims as the field scrolls under the proxy.
        //
        // No path is lost: the clamp below pulls the last step to the exit, so every ray
        // covers the same span whatever its offset.
        if (i == 0)
        {
            h *= firstScale;
        }

        if (t + h > tExit)
        {
            h = tExit - t;
        }

        // SAMPLED AT THE MIDPOINT. Growing steps otherwise weight each interval by its
        // LEADING FACE: the density found at the near edge is applied across the whole
        // step, and since every step is longer than the one before it, the error is
        // systematic rather than noise -- the field reads heavy in the direction of
        // travel, and more so the faster the steps grow. The galaxy march samples the
        // midpoint for the same reason.
        //
        // It costs the step length being needed first, which is why the sizing above
        // moved ahead of the fetch.
        float3 samplePos = o + dir * (t + h * 0.5);

        float density = P.SampleAtPosition(InTex, InSampler, samplePos);
        if (bShape)
        {
            density = P.PowSafe(density, InNoisePower);
        }

        // THE BOUNDS FADE IS APPLIED HERE, not inside the field. It is a property of how
        // the field is being viewed rather than of the field, and this is the only place
        // that knows about the sphere it fades toward -- entity generation samples the
        // same core and must see none of it. dot(samplePos, samplePos) is the same
        // radial term the core used to compute for itself; the marched position is
        // already in the normalized frame the fade is defined in.
        density *= P.BoundsFade(dot(samplePos, samplePos));

        float alpha = 1.0 - exp(-density * InDensityScale * h * 0.5);

        // Flat white emission: structure only.
        emission += alpha * transmittance;
        transmittance *= 1.0 - alpha;

        if (transmittance < 0.001)
        {
            break;
        }

        t += h;
    }

    return float4(emission, stepsTaken, 0.0, transmittance);
}
